 "description": "A high-speed line (schema 65, decision 168), 37 steps. A straight road 61 km long with three stations on it: A at its west end, N 5 km along (numbered 3) and B 61 km along (numbered 2), platforms of 128 m, and the line Fast A-N-B, all day, every ten minutes with three trains; network routing. A trip from A to B pays the network's flat $5 (500 cents). Planning the line at PERF_HSR makes it a high-speed line (there is no other switch; an unknown line is refused). The stations are given hinterlands, which the land would otherwise count: A 2,000,000 people (homes), N 500,000 (shops) and B 1,000,000 (offices), none with trips of their own. Between two high-speed stations the intercity trips of a day, one way, are K x a x b with K = 2,400 for a million and a million, in millions and rounded down a step at a time, kept by a share of the straight-line distance (none up to 10 km, all from 80 km, in a straight line between), rounded half up: A to B, 61 km, 2 x 1 x 2,400 = 4,800, 728 thousandths of it = 3,494 (1000 x (3,904,000 - 640,000) / 4,480,000 = 728.6); N to B, 56 km, 0.5 x 1 x 2,400 = 1,200, 657 thousandths = 788; A to N, 5 km, none. Both ways the same. The fastest routes take under the half hour a trip is kept whole for (three trains, every ten minutes), so the minutes take nothing off. The hours are the day's shape times the origin's departure peak and the destination's arrival peak (tools/golden-checks/high_speed.py works every value out from the rules). A trip that rides the line pays the high-speed fare, round(km x .45) dollars for the straight-line distance, if that is more than the network's $5: A to B 61 km = 27.45, $27 = 2,700 cents; A to N 5 km = 2.25, under the network's 500; N to B 56 km = 25.2, $25 = 2,500; without the line, 500.",
